{"authored" [{"question" "q-81063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a375ee6ee6aed3cd4a02d9a41385a1beb1356ae26e4b0633f09eef4476b2e5" "basis" 1237 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(1)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(1)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a375ee6ee6aed3cd4a02d9a41385a1beb1356ae26e4b0633f09eef4476b2e5" "basis" 1237 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "