"attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=4+3(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{′}(-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=-1,$ so the coefficient of the first-degree term is ${f}^{′}(-1).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the first-degree term is $3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-83725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-347994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=5+3(x-2)-7(x-2)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{′}(2)?$\n\n${f}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-347994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=2,$ so the coefficient of the first-degree term is ${f}^{′}(2).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the first-degree term is $3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-347994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-347994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-347994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-10$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=7-8(x+10)+15(x+10)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-10)?$\n\n${f}^{″}(-10)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=-10,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-10).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $15.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-10)=15\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-10)=30.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-349356" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349356" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "30"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-50948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=1+6(x-3)+18(x-3)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-50948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=3,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $18.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3)=18\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(3)=36.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-50948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-50948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-50948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "18"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "38"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-50948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "36"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=9$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=-1+4(x-9)-13(x-9)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(9)?$\n\n${f}^{″}(9)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=9,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(9).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-13.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(9)=-13\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(9)=-26.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-349354" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-349354" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-26"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/bb52a7975c917c7fe310eb29ae1daaa3438a906b84771ae5ce6998c055c4e6f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(3)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-83728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1+2(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}"] ["math" "-2-3(x-3)-2(x-3)^{2}"] ["math" "2-2(x-3)+3(x-3)^{2}"] ["math" "3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}"] ["math" "4+3(x-3)+3(x-3)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(1)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(1)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5+2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}"] ["math" "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}"] ["math" "-5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}"] ["math" "5+2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}"] ["math" "5-2(x-1)-6(x-1)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-81063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/9fe49372324dac217bb8e96ae6f485fe74308184ec9dd197fc98b973560dd8af.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-83729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3+3(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "-4-2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "2+(x+2)+2(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "3(x+2)-2(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-83729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-125822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/53b9bfe6c2b3109a14fa42651fe402fc546f6dceb1526459500fbdf537565976.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-125822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-125822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-125822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-125822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1+3(x+2)-5(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "1+3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "1+3(x+2)-5(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "1-3(x+2)-5(x+2)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-125822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-15523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/ef5618f26d499eeffd693d1f0dc674761bcfd5762b9620044b295870983799a0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f.$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-15523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-3)}{2}(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-3)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-15523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6da26a6fc4547b0de25b0883a61c0af2facdd4ce5b254fa329f64612c64adf8"} {"question" "q-15523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-15523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2+(x+3)+(x+3)^{2}"] ["math" "-2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}"] ["math" "2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}"] ["math" "2+x+{x}^{2}"] ["math" "2+x-{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "q-15523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/state.json" "file_sha256" "e70bb8a7f5017b3afe2233ee2e9ed33decf7160dc754cfea4308fa65ab1fadb2"} {"question" "e-738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/example-738.json" "file_sha256" "9aabe4cfacff66f8a2eb0b9fbfd0320b7a3ce28b57d81e3d0beae0ad51a2d23d"} {"question" "e-738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an expansion about the point $x=2,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/example-738.json" "file_sha256" "9aabe4cfacff66f8a2eb0b9fbfd0320b7a3ce28b57d81e3d0beae0ad51a2d23d"} {"question" "e-739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/8b630e44d49440844d668f395dd5d85676e1824f283edb86194a88a62c78b3f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1?$\n\n- $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/example-739.json" "file_sha256" "9615267eed557c80b6483c05b7f37f1bf90abb883f74f08049657addcdf2cf7d"} {"question" "e-739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)  <0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- $f'(-1) >0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- $f''(-1) <0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/example-739.json" "file_sha256" "9615267eed557c80b6483c05b7f37f1bf90abb883f74f08049657addcdf2cf7d"}] "usage" {"q-15523" [] "q-81063" [] "e-739" [] "e-738" []} "basis" 1321 "retractions" [[17592186294399 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$"] [17592186184472 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3825/Source/Images/q-15523.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x =-3$?"] [17592186184029 :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$f(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2$.\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)$?"] [17592186184029 :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=2$, so the coefficient of the second-degree term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2)\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24\n$$"] [17592186184030 :question/problem "The figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x)$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3825/Source/Images/e67c9dfab10704ddb8dfa9e9559f5e0d.png)\n\n1. $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n2. $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n3. $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$"] [17592186184030 :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1) <0$. Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n- $f'(-1) >0$, because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n- $f''(-1) <0$, because the graph is concave down at $x=-1$. Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186181281 17592186181282 17592186181283 17592186181284 17592186181285 17592186294401 17592186294402 17592186294403 17592186294404 17592186294405] "immutable_fields" [[17592186181280 :answer-field/correct] [17592186181280 :answer-field/id] [17592186181280 :answer-field/key] [17592186181280 :answer-field/type] [17592186294400 :answer-field/correct] [17592186294400 :answer-field/id] [17592186294400 :answer-field/key] [17592186294400 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
